# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

.vercel
card-art-uploads/
# Local asset tooling (slicers, uploaders) stays out of the repo, but ANYTHING
# prebuild runs MUST ship or every Vercel build dies at
# ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND while passing perfectly well locally, because the file
# is right there on disk and only missing from the commit.
#
# AND THE ALLOW-LIST DRIFTED, exactly as an allow-list does. It named five
# scripts by hand while `npm run check` grew to ten, so four of the checks were
# only ever on one laptop -- including the one that was about to be fixed. CI
# went red on its first run: ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND, passing perfectly well
# locally, which is the failure this comment was already warning about.
#
# So the rule is structural now instead of a list somebody has to remember:
# EVERY check-*.mts ships, by pattern. A new check cannot be forgotten because
# nothing has to be added anywhere. The one-off tooling (slicers, uploaders,
# placers) is what this section is actually for and it stays out.
#
# Note the glob: `scripts/` would ignore the directory itself, and git will not
# descend into an ignored directory, so a `!scripts/...` negation under it does
# nothing. It has to be `scripts/*`.
scripts/*
!scripts/check-*.mts
!scripts/derive-exchange-payouts.mjs

# Badge slicer source sheets (dev-only input for slice-badges.mjs; sliced output in public/badges/ is committed)
# Badge SOURCE sheets. Multi-badge plates that slice-badges.mjs cuts into
# public/badges/. Source art, never served, so they live outside public/ and
# stay out of the repo. The old public/ path is kept so an existing checkout
# does not suddenly start tracking 85MB of them.
art-source/badgebatch*.png
public/badgebatch*.png
.git-commit-msg.tmp

# public/sea/ USED to be the Godot web export for the /sea page — a build
# artifact, hence the ignore. That experiment is parked (the ocean hub is
# painted 2D now, see app/(app)/sea), and the directory holds the chart's
# building art instead, which absolutely must ship. The rule stayed behind after
# the pivot and silently swallowed the first five plates dropped into it.
# If the Godot export ever comes back it needs its own path.

# Nano-banana prompt sources for generated art. The PNG/JPG that comes out is
# committed; the prompt that made it is kept locally so a regeneration starts
# from the same words rather than from memory.

# Nano-banana SHEET sources for the crew ladders. One generation is a 3x2 grid
# at 2K; art/cut-crew-sheets.mjs cuts it into the six plates in public/crew/,
# and those are what ship. The sheets are a few MB each and never served, so
# they stay local the way the badge plates do.
art/raw/
art/backup/
